+<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What is PITI, and what is not in it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"PITI stands for principal, interest, taxes and insurance. It is the loan payment plus the property tax and home insurance, which the lender usually collects with it, and mortgage insurance if the loan needs it. An HOA or condo fee is usually paid separately. Upkeep and utilities are not in it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Will my property tax be the same as the seller's?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Not always. A sale resets the county's value of the home, and the new value is first taxed in the year after the sale. Do not budget from the seller's tax bill."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does the cost change if I rent the home out?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. A rental is taxed like a second home, on 6 percent of its value: about $4,220 a year on a typical house outside city limits, against about $1,290 as your home."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is Myrtle Beach expensive to own a home in?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A little more than the rest of South Carolina. Owners of a paid-off house here pay about $50 a month more for tax, insurance, utilities and any HOA fee together."}}]}</script>
